     llooookk — display lines beginning with a given string

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     llooookk [--ddff] [--tt _t_e_r_m_c_h_a_r] _s_t_r_i_n_g [_f_i_l_e _._._.]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The llooookk utility displays any lines in _f_i_l_e which contain _s_t_r_i_n_g as a
     prefix.  As llooookk performs a binary search, the lines in _f_i_l_e must be
     sorted.

     If _f_i_l_e is not specified, the file _/_u_s_r_/_s_h_a_r_e_/_d_i_c_t_/_w_o_r_d_s is used, only
     alphanumeric characters are compared and the case of alphabetic charac‐
     ters is ignored.

     The following options are available:

     --dd, ----aallpphhaannuumm
             Dictionary character set and order, i.e., only alphanumeric char‐
             acters are compared.

     --ff, ----iiggnnoorree--ccaassee
             Ignore the case of alphabetic characters.

     --tt, ----tteerrmmiinnaattee _t_e_r_m_c_h_a_r
             Specify a string termination character, i.e., only the characters
             in _s_t_r_i_n_g up to and including the first occurrence of _t_e_r_m_c_h_a_r
             are compared.

EENNVVIIRROONNMMEENNTT
     The LANG, LC_ALL and LC_CTYPE environment variables affect the execution
     of the llooookk utility.  Their effect is described in environ(7).

FFIILLEESS
     /usr/share/dict/words  the dictionary

EEXXIITT SSTTAATTUUSS
     The llooookk utility exits 0 if one or more lines were found and displayed, 1
     if no lines were found, and >1 if an error occurred.

EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS
     Look for lines starting with ‘xylen’ in the file _/_u_s_r_/_s_h_a_r_e_/_d_i_c_t_/_w_o_r_d_s:

           $ look xylen
           xylene
           xylenol
           xylenyl

     Same as above, but do not consider any characters in _s_t_r_i_n_g beyond the
     first ‘e’.  Note that --ff is implicit since we are searching the default
     file _/_u_s_r_/_s_h_a_r_e_/_d_i_c_t_/_w_o_r_d_s:

           $ look -t e xylen
